From c83ef858a7947c7e7e2cfa69d7fe44bf197a484e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: William Emerison Six <billsix@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 8 Sep 2026 03:25:28 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 343/488] soh: name the 16 address-named symbols defined in
 z_en_sda.c

SoH carries the Ocarina of Time decomp, in which several thousand symbols are
still named after their ROM address. This commit names every such symbol DEFINED
IN ONE FILE, so the work can be reviewed a file at a time.

The file names the unit; it does not bound it. Each rename is applied wherever
the symbol occurs -- the declaration in the header, the definition, and every
call site -- so this commit reaches into whatever other files use these symbols
and leaves no dangling reference. It is a pure rename: no behaviour changes, and
the only edits that are not renames are the provenance comments described below.

Two name sources are used, and every name says which it came from:

  * zeldaret/oot -- SoH's decomp IS zeldaret/oot at the same ROM addresses, so
    where oot has already named a symbol, that name is adopted verbatim. The
    definition gets a "Name from zeldaret/oot" comment and every declaration a
    short "// was <addr> [oot]" tag.

  * DEDUCED -- oot leaves a great many symbols address-named too, so those
    names are inferred from the body and its callers, and are marked as OURS
    rather than passed off as upstream. The definition gets
    "LLM generated name (HIGH|GUESS), was <addr>: <reason>" and the short tag
    reads "[LLM:HIGH]" or "[LLM:GUESS]". A GUESS is tagged at every call site
    as well, not only the declaration, so a reader who meets the name anywhere
    knows it is inferred. "git grep '[LLM:'" lists every place the decomp
    leans on an inference.

The justification recorded for each individual name follows, one entry per
symbol, in the order the renames were made. Nothing is summarised away: these
are the per-commit reasons from the unsquashed history, with the boilerplate
that was identical in every one of them hoisted into the text above.

All 16 are defined in soh/src/overlays/actors/ovl_En_Sda/z_en_sda.c.

  * D_80AFA660 -> sBodyPartPos
    deduced, confidence HIGH: the sixteen body-part positions copied out of
    the player each frame and used as the centres of the shadow blobs.

  * D_80AFA16C -> sBodyPartToSlot
    deduced, confidence HIGH: maps each player body-part index to its slot
    in sBodyPartPos, with -1 for the parts that are not used.

  * D_80AFA13C -> sBodyPartLinks
    deduced, confidence HIGH: for each slot, the slot it is joined to; the
    stamping pass interpolates between the pair so limbs cast a continuous
    shadow.

  * D_80AFA15C -> sBlobSizeClass
    deduced, confidence HIGH: which of the five brush-width tables each
    body-part slot stamps with.

  * D_80AFA0E8 -> sBlobRowsClass0
    deduced, confidence HIGH: the per-row half-widths of the brush used for
    size class 0.

  * D_80AFA0F8 -> sBlobRowsClass1
    deduced, confidence HIGH: the per-row half-widths of the brush used for
    size class 1.

  * D_80AFA108 -> sBlobRowsClass2
    deduced, confidence HIGH: the per-row half-widths of the brush used for
    size class 2.

  * D_80AFA0DC -> sBlobRowsClass3
    deduced, confidence HIGH: the per-row half-widths of the smallest brush,
    used for size class 3.

  * D_80AFA120 -> sBlobRowsClass4
    deduced, confidence HIGH: the per-row half-widths of the largest brush,
    used for size class 4.

  * D_80AFA0D0 -> sZeroVec
    deduced, confidence HIGH: a zero Vec3f transformed by the current matrix
    to get the shadow's origin.

  * D_80AFA180 -> sBlobOffsets
    deduced, confidence HIGH: the twenty-two offsets the second stamping
    pass smears the shadow across, each with its own depth weight.

  * D_80AFA288 -> sUnusedBlobOffsets
    deduced, confidence HIGH: the in-tree comment above it reads "Unused,
    identical to D_80AFA180".

  * D_80AFA390 -> sUnusedValues
    deduced, confidence HIGH: two zeroed u32 words with no reader anywhere
    in the tree.

  * func_80AF8F60 -> EnSda_StampBodyShadow
    deduced, confidence HIGH: stamps the player's sixteen body-part
    positions into the shadow texture, interpolating each along its link by
    the caller's weight and picking the brush from its size class.

  * func_80AF95C4 -> EnSda_BuildShadowTexture
    deduced, confidence HIGH: fills sBodyPartPos from the player, stretches
    the head slot outward and then runs several stamping passes at
    increasing weights to build the soft shadow.

  * func_80AF9C70 -> EnSda_DrawShadow
    deduced, confidence HIGH: draws the finished shadow texture flat on the
    floor under the player, tinted by the debug REGs.

